---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { nOwn, nInherited } from "../lib/errors.js";
import reg from "../data/register.json";
import dossiers from "../data/dossiers.json";
import searched from "../data/searched.json";
import { people } from "../lib/people.js";

/* Every number on this page is computed. A page that says what the archive
   holds must not be the one page whose figures go stale. */
const out = (o) => searched.rows.filter((r) => r.outcome === o).length;
const HOLDS = [
  [people.length, "people published", "of a tree that also holds 2,034 living people, none of them here", "/people"],
  [dossiers.counts.named, "named in records", `including ${dossiers.counts.ownPage} who are not relatives at all`, "/who"],
  [reg.entries, "register entries", `${reg.records} from a document, ${reg.tree} from the tree`, "/register"],
  [searched.rows.length, "sources read", `${out("yield")} yields, ${out("null")} nulls, ${out("blocked")} unreachable`, "/searched"],
  [nOwn, "errors corrected in public", `plus ${nInherited} inherited from the tree`, "/corrections"],
  ["≈7", "generations it will defend", "not the twelve the tree claims", "/how-far-back"],
];
---
<Base title="About" eyebrow="Who built this, and what it is willing to say"
  dek="An evidence-first archive of the D'Arcys of Brisbane, kept to the same method as five sibling archives — and the rules it will not break to make a better story.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">This archive exists because a family had a tree that reached the kings of Denmark
    and nobody had ever asked it a hard question. It is one of <strong>six</strong>, kept to the same
    method and by the same hand. <a href={u("/the-other-archives/")}>The other five →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What it is willing to say, and what it is not</h2>
    <ul class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <li style="margin-bottom:.6rem"><strong>A family tree is not a source.</strong> It is a set of
      assertions. Of {reg.entries} entries in the register, {reg.records} rest on a document; the rest rest on
      somebody's tree, and every page says which is which.
      <a href={u("/register/")}>The register →</a></li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.6rem"><strong>A null result is a result.</strong> Every source read
      and found empty is published, so the next person does not repeat the search.
      <a href={u("/searched/")}>What has been read →</a></li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.6rem"><strong>Errors stay on the page where they were made.</strong>
      {nOwn} so far, each with what caused it. Corrections are never made silently.
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>The errors →</a></li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.6rem"><strong>Living people are not published at all.</strong> Not
      hidden, not gated — absent from the build. The rule withholds 2,034 people across the tree,
      including their names wherever they would appear as somebody's spouse, parent or child.
      <a href={u("/method/")}>How the rule works →</a></li>
      <li><strong>A better story is not a reason.</strong> Two aristocratic descents have been taken
      off this family — one unevidenced, one disproved — and both are still set out in full rather
      than deleted. <a href={u("/how-far-back/")}>Both grafts →</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What it holds</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          {HOLDS.map(([n, what, note, href]) => (
            <tr><td><a href={u(href)}>{what}</a></td><td class="m"><strong>{n}</strong></td>
                <td class="small">{note}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Audited 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">This archive had drifted to the wrong side of the world</h2>

    <p class="lede">It exists to test a <strong>British</strong> ancestry: a Gloucestershire yeoman,
    a Staffordshire weaver, a Royal Engineer at Chatham. When this was first counted the archive held
    <strong>95 Australian records against 70 British</strong> — 57% of its evidence from the wrong
    side of the world.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Where the evidence comes from</th><th class="m">Records</th><th class="m">Share</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Australian — Queensland registration, QSA, NAA, AWM, Trove, the Blue Books</td>
              <td class="m">98</td><td class="m">36%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>British — FreeREG, FreeBMD, the censuses, TNA, Gloucestershire Archives, the Army Lists, the London Gazette</td>
              <td class="m">176</td><td class="m">64%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>There is a good reason and it is not a good enough reason.</strong> Australian records
      are open: Queensland's death index names parents and is free, its archives publish scans, its
      immigration lists are open data. British records are scattered, half-transcribed and — three
      times in one day — behind a bot check. The path of least resistance runs east, and this archive
      followed it.
      <br /><br />
      The result is that the best-evidenced part of a British family history is its emigration. That
      is worth knowing, and worth correcting.
      <br /><br />
      <strong>It has been corrected.</strong> Since the audit was published, <strong>eighty-seven
      British records have been added and one Australian one</strong> — the Berkeley estate deeds,
      the Army List of 1778, a Welsh will, a Bristol census household, nine medieval deeds naming
      people <em>of Saniger</em>, Bigland's monumental inscriptions, two Gloucestershire wills, a
      Hanley census, and a line in the London Gazette of 1813.
      <br /><br />
      <strong>On 13 September 2026 the archive crossed over.</strong> British evidence now
      outnumbers Australian — <strong>176 to 98</strong>, 64% against 36%, having started at 42%
      against 57%. That is not a change of subject; it is the same family, evidenced from the
      country it came from. The measure is kept here so the next correction can be measured too.
      <br /><br />
      <a href={u("/searched/")}>Every source, and which country it is in →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>How it is built</h2>
    <p class="dek">A MyHeritage GEDCOM export is parsed in Python, living people are removed by a
    graph-wide rule rather than a flag, and the result is rendered as a static site with Astro and
    published to GitHub Pages. Nothing is generated at request time and there is no database —
    everything you can read here is in the repository, including the tools that built it.</p>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.9rem">
      <a href="https://github.com/daviddef/TheDArcy">The repository ↗</a> ·
      <a href={u("/method/")}>Method →</a> · <a href={u("/sources/")}>Sources →</a> ·
      <a href={u("/changes/")}>What changed →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <div class="note"><strong>A correction is the most valuable thing anyone can send this
    archive.</strong> If something here is wrong — a date, a place, a join, a person who should not
    be published — say so, and it will be changed, and the change will be recorded on
    <a href={u("/changes/")}>what changed</a> rather than quietly applied.</div>
  </section>
</Base>
